{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RE-INVESTIGATION (Phase 1 redone at operator request: \"root-cause at source, do NOT raise the timeout blind\"). The earlier RCA proved the timeout *fires* with a synthetic `sleep 3s` commit shim, but never established WHY a real `git commit` exceeds 30s — it concluded \"host is just slow, raise 30->120 (Option A)\". Re-run of Phase 1 finds a genuine performance/correctness DEFECT at the source; the bound stays 30s.\n\n## Proven root cause (deeper than \"30 is too small\")\nOn git >= 2.47, `git commit` runs `git maintenance run --auto` automatically. ADR 0051 (bug 88eb, commit 27e1165cdbbc, 2026-07-18) deliberately forces that maintenance FOREGROUND on the tickets worktree (`_init_ensures._gc_config_unit`: `--unset gc.auto` + `gc.autoDetach=false` + `maintenance.autoDetach=false`) so the repack runs serialized under the store write lock instead of racing writers (the concurrent-gc corruption 88eb fixed). Consequence: the O(store) auto-repack runs *synchronously inside the lock-held `git commit` subprocess* — the exact subprocess `event_commit_git._run_git` bounds with `_GIT_TIMEOUT=30` (event_commit_git.py:61; `_git_commit` at :101). `gc.auto` is left at git's default (~6700 loose objects), so once a store accumulates that many loose objects, the *triggering* commit pays the full foreground repack cost. On a large/mature store that repack exceeds 30s and the commit is SIGKILLed: the write is lost AND the kill lands mid-`git repack` — precisely the interrupted-maintenance corruption hazard ADR 0051 exists to prevent (cf. the already-present self-heal `event_append._recover_from_invalid_object`, bug 4c1c: \"an index entry whose object VANISHED — a gc repack ... under pressure\").\n\nThe 30s is not merely mis-sized — a batch O(store) maintenance op must not be charged to a per-commit LATENCY bound at all. Raising 30->120 (Option A) only moves the cliff (a bigger store exceeds 120s) and still SIGKILLs mid-repack.\n\n## Discriminating experiments (runtime, git 2.55.0, rebar gc config, isolated repos)\n1. THRESHOLD TRIGGER — one real commit, loose objects just below vs. just above 6700:\n   - 5 loose: commit wall = 0.087s.\n   - after adding 7000 loose (cross ~6700): SAME commit wall = 6.533s, loose count -> 0 (GIT_TRACE2 shows the maintenance/repack child inside the commit).\n   - next commit (loose reset): 0.084s.\n   => the >30s cost is a specific, deterministically threshold-triggered foreground repack, NOT diffuse host load.\n2. SCALING — 8 cycles of (add 7500 loose, commit) on a growing store: maintenance-commit wall grows with store size (2.0s @ 7.5k in-pack -> 2.5s @ 60k in-pack / 7 MiB). O(store); a mature store (rebar's mandated many-concurrent-agents mode) crosses 30s.\n3. FIX DISCRIMINATOR (ARM A vs ARM B), 7500 loose objects present:\n   - ARM A plain `git commit`: wall 1.967s, loose_after=0 (maintenance fired inside commit).\n   - ARM B `git -c gc.auto=0 -c maintenance.auto=false commit`: wall 0.036s, loose_after=7503 (maintenance SUPPRESSED on this commit; objects DEFERRED, not lost).\n   => suppressing auto-maintenance on the commit makes it O(1)-fast regardless of loose count.\n4. DEFERRED MAINTENANCE STILL WORKS — explicit `git maintenance run --auto`: no-op below threshold (0.046s, loose unchanged), repacks above threshold (2.2s, loose -> 0). So maintenance can run as its own budgeted step.\n\nRivals rejected: \"host generically slow\" (cost is the specific repack child, threshold-gated, not proportional host load); \"network push counted in commit\" (commit is `--no-verify`, no remote in the isolated store, and push is a separate post-lock step); \"store-lock acquire loss\" (royal-weariless-zebrafish / 10c3-5a33 is a LockTimeout BEFORE the body; here the lock is already held).\n\n## Legitimacy: DEFECT\nViolates two cited authorities: (a) the design intent that `_GIT_TIMEOUT` is a per-op latency watchdog on a *commit* (c2ba docstring \"bound every lock-held git child\"; push.py parity), and (b) ADR 0051's own invariant that maintenance completes serialized under the lock — a 30s SIGKILL mid-repack breaks it.\n\n## Defect origin (caused_by) — c2ba, class=regression\nUnmet-obligation, dated: ADR 0051 (foreground maintenance) landed 2026-07-18; c2ba (`3aec0ae90ab`, rebar-ticket c2ba-98bf-ca2d-481a, 2026-08-01) added `_GIT_TIMEOUT=30` to the lock-held commit 13 days LATER, over a commit that already carried the foreground-repack obligation, choosing a network-grade value copied from push.py. Before c2ba the lock-held git children were unbounded, so a slow-but-healthy foreground repack completed -> a later change broke working behavior = regression. Origin = c2ba (caused_by already linked). Contributing miss: 9305/icicled-unstable-urial recalibrated the SIBLING seam to `gitutil._LOCAL_GIT_TIMEOUT=120` (and moved txn/claim onto it) but left event_commit_git at 30 — so the claim/transition write surface is the parity sibling of this bug.\n\nRelationship to prior tickets (AC-5): moldavite-evasive-elephant (9590-34f5) recorded the first occurrence (mis-classified as runner I/O); royal-weariless-zebrafish (10c3-5a33) is pre-lock acquire, a distinct mechanism. Both already linked.\n\n## Proposed fix (bound STAYS 30s; c2ba parity test untouched) — NOTE: reverses ticket's \"Option A approved\" per current operator directive\nDecouple auto-maintenance from the latency-bounded write commit on BOTH lock-held write surfaces (event_commit_git + txn claim/transition): run the commit with `-c gc.auto=0 -c maintenance.auto=false` so the 30s bound covers only the commit, then run maintenance as an explicit `git maintenance run --auto` step under the SAME write lock (ADR 0051 serialization preserved) bounded by the watchdog-grade `_LOCAL_GIT_TIMEOUT`, not the per-commit latency bound. Keeps `_GIT_TIMEOUT=30` and `tests/unit/test_store_write_git_timeout_c2ba.py` intact; removes the O(store)-op-under-latency-bound defect and the SIGKILL-mid-repack corruption hazard. Regression test converts experiment 3 into RED->GREEN. Proceeding to Phase 2 under RED->GREEN per operator directive #1 (genuine source defect -> remediate, timeout stays 30s)."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787957035646571001,"uuid":"12da6190-b5db-47f9-93cc-b6eeaa47a0e1"}